        [: `I32, Error!`]
        [: shared]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `image_data_set_pixel`]
        [: `image_data ~CanvasImageData, x I32, y I32, red I32, green I32, blue I32, alpha I32`]
        [: `Error!`]
        [: mutable]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `image_data_clear_pixel`]
        [: `image_data ~CanvasImageData, x I32, y I32`]
        [: `Error!`]
        [: mutable]
    ]
]

Pixel getters and setters fail with `Error!` when coordinates fall outside the image data bounds. Channel values clamp to 0 through 255.

### Access and return contracts

Functions that mutate the context take `~Canvas2d` (mutable access). Query functions take `Canvas2d` (shared access). The same pattern applies to `~CanvasElement`, `~CanvasGradient` and `~CanvasImageData`. Fresh values from creation functions satisfy mutable parameters without `~`. Existing places require explicit `~` at the call site.

All parameters stay positional-only. Named arguments are not supported because binding-backed packages do not expose source-function features.

### Error codes

Fallible canvas functions return builtin `Error` values whose `code` names a host-operation category. The compiler owns these codes and `canvas.js` imports them by name from `@moth/runtime`.

[table:
    [th:
        [: Code]
        [: Category]
        [: Canvas failures]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `400`]
        [: Invalid host argument]
        [: Pixel coordinates outside the image data bounds]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `404`]
        [: Host resource not found]
        [: No `<canvas>` or `<img>` element with the requested id]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `409`]
        [: Host resource unavailable]
        [: An image that hasn't finished loading or is broken, or a pattern the browser can't create]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `500`]
        [: Host operation failed]
        [: A missing 2D context or a browser exception. The message is the exception's message when it has one.]
    ]
]

These are host-operation categories, not HTTP status semantics.

### Opaque type rules

- Do not construct opaque types with struct syntax.
- Do not inspect opaque type fields.
- Opaque types have no source-visible methods. Use the free functions.
- The `@html` package provides a `Canvas` wrapper struct with receiver methods for a more ergonomic drawing API.

### Backend behaviour

The JS backend fully supports `@web/canvas`. When canvas functions are reachable, the build emits the `canvas.js` runtime asset, a generated glue module and an import map entry for `@moth/runtime`.

The Wasm backend rejects reachable `@web/canvas` calls before lowering. Canvas support requires a browser JS environment.

### Teardown

`@web/canvas` defines no teardown, close or disposal functions. Opaque handles are managed by the host JavaScript runtime.

### Unsupported source forms

- Direct symbol-path clauses such as `@web/canvas/get_canvas` face rejection. Use direct selections or a namespace clause.
- Named arguments are not supported. Binding-backed calls stay positional-only.
- The package does not expose receiver methods, source-defined types or constants.
- Opaque types cannot be constructed or inspected from Moth source.

### Deferred surfaces

`@web/canvas` ships as a skeleton. Deferred surfaces include async image-loading helpers, `toBlob` and `captureStream`, raw typed-array and arbitrary line-dash array access, `Path2D`, `DOMMatrix`, `OffscreenCanvas`, `ImageBitmap` and video sources. These will arrive as the language and external package ABI mature. WebGL belongs to @../graphics (@web/graphics), not this package. See @../../../progress (progress matrix) for current status.

### Related concepts

- For package origin and backing see @../../#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing).
- For dependency-path rules see @../../#dependency-paths (Dependency paths).
- For the `@html` source-backed wrapper see @../html (@html).
